==English==

===Etymology===
Probably a {{back-form|en|shapeshifter|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʃeɪp.ʃɪft/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Sapaa-shapeshift.wav|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|biology|fantasy|mythology|intransitive}} To change one's [[shape]] or [[form]] (into another) through often [[magical]] means, normally applied only to living beings.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=Follow the Shaman's Call: An Ancient Path for Modern Lives |author=Mike Williams | page=91| passage=Journey in exactly the same way as you did to '''shapeshift''' into your power animal except, this time, set your intention to '''shapeshift''' into a named ancestor. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| title=Gateway To Brienka|author=Emily Rayven | page=| passage=I can't '''shapeshift''' Zane, I only look like this I always have.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=Entangled Life|author=Merlin Sheldrake| page=57| passage=But to embed oneself in an irregular and unpredictable food supply as mycelium does, one must be able to '''shapeshift'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|intransitive}} To [[undergo]] a noticeable [[change]] (in character for example), to [[metamorphosize]].
#* '''2019''', Adam Turner-Heffer, Chelsea Wolfe: ''Birth of Violence'' review, ''The Skinny'', September 2019, [https://issuu.com/theskinny/docs/the_skinny_september_2019 page 57]:
#*: While Chelsea Wolfe has often '''shapeshifted''' in terms of how she presents her music, one thing remains constant: her big mood.

